 ## Overview
-Ray Traversal and Integration Schemes define the computational framework for solving the volume rendering equation by determining where to sample a volume function $V(\mathbf{p})$ along a camera ray and how to aggregate those samples into a final pixel color. This family solves the problem of synthesizing images from volumetric data (textures, implicit fields, or neural representations) by approximating the continuous integral of light transport. It sets itself apart from [Monte Carlo Volume Rendering](2060-monte-carlo-volume-rendering.md) by typically employing deterministic quadrature (fixed or adaptive steps) rather than stochastic sampling, and from [Pre-integration and Approximation Methods](12352-pre-integration-and-approximation-methods.md) by performing the integration directly in the rendering loop without relying on pre-computed auxiliary volumes like lookup tables.
+Ray Traversal and Integration Schemes define the computational framework for evaluating the volume rendering integral by determining where to sample volumetric data along a camera ray and how to aggregate those samples into a final pixel color. This family solves the problem of discretizing the continuous light transport equation for real-time or high-fidelity rendering, distinguishing itself from object-order methods by iterating per-pixel and from pure Monte Carlo methods by often using deterministic traversal or structured aggregation . It encompasses the fundamental loop structures (Image-order, Ray Casting), the sampling strategies (Ray Marching, Cone Casting, Subdividing), and the aggregation logic (Front-to-Back, LOS Integration, Ray Traversal Schemes) [S1, unofficial].
 
