 ## Background
-The concept of ray casting for volume rendering was introduced by Levoy in 1989, establishing the foundation for image-order rendering by treating the volume as a participating medium [G]. The term "ray marching" became prevalent with the rise of GPU-based real-time rendering, allowing for the iterative evaluation of signed distance fields (SDFs) and volumetric textures. The specific integration schemes described here, such as front-to-back compositing, are derived from the volume rendering equation formalized by Kajiya and von Herzen (1989) [G]. Adaptive segment length strategies, such as those discussed by Quatrin Campagnolo et al., were developed to address the trade-off between rendering speed and visual fidelity, particularly in mitigating "onion ring" artifacts caused by equidistant sampling [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of ray casting for volume visualization was introduced by Levoy in 1989, establishing the image-order loop and front-to-back compositing as the standard for direct volume rendering [G]. Ray marching techniques evolved to support real-time performance on GPUs, leveraging fragment shaders for parallel execution . Adaptive sampling and subdivision techniques were developed to address aliasing artifacts caused by perspective projection, where ray density decreases with distance [S1, unofficial]. Monte Carlo methods for volumes, such as Woodcock tracking, were adapted from particle transport theory to handle participating media with varying extinction coefficients efficiently [S1, unofficial]. Line-of-Sight (LOS) integration has seen renewed interest in scientific visualization, particularly for solar physics and neural radiance fields, where physical quantities are integrated directly rather than rendered as color [S1, unofficial].
 
